New toplevel windows are filled with their theme background until the first
frame is drawn.

GDK creates a toplevel's X window with no background, so until the program
draws its first frame the server leaves whatever was on the screen there.  A
program that takes a moment to start -- a browser setting up its web
process -- appeared for half a second as an empty frame you could see
through.  At realize the window's X background is set to the window's own
background colour from the theme (when it is opaque, the window is not
app-paintable and uses the default visual), so the server fills it the
moment it is mapped; after the first frame it goes back to none, and later
exposures are repainted the way they always were.

